#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "e7fb27f0-67b3-58fa-a582-acd4a4ebe045" :question/math-academy-id "e-5588" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/fa49e62c4e6fce14470e1641ef5e9a8c986778d74812e1bac82d3ba9df94aef8.png)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9f8a0e56-d0c5-56dd-9204-c13922ec8ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5589" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/8b006c23e83405be4cd93396e5bde38c0dbcb5f8bb735b125d0b05f439e5b0d9.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/6fed2ac5452cf11059550a0c6328914c0e897714ca14a68f71df8eeb6d93471d.png)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "af2b3f92-fed5-5a50-b662-eaadd9b7adbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7429" :question/problem "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/8200befaaea3a0cdec7e423999839c72d48fc67110fa577c8707e182c4524aff.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when $\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12dca220-38f5-54f1-bedb-838ff36621e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369572} {:answer/id #uuid "b08a6422-cbd1-5495-ae5d-e92b9de153d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369573} {:answer/id #uuid "8de3dfee-e457-5fe8-b107-b1f531721bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369574} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a08dcf-c960-5868-a79b-2cd84dad87eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369575} {:answer/id #uuid "016ea5e6-cead-5a32-a240-53efcc5f310a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a08dcf-c960-5868-a79b-2cd84dad87eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369575} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a7dd940-0683-5c5d-bc55-8e280acb921a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eac623d-3625-5eef-86a3-1e868caeb2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40887" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97f3056b-45d1-59d8-9a7a-c18312268181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369579} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd6caad-3536-5b06-8e20-f4abcc84702d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369580} {:answer/id #uuid "0622ee20-cb57-540d-b982-16a006736a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369581} {:answer/id #uuid "0aac813e-594e-510f-82d4-65383abfdb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369582} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c27974-80da-51ef-89de-28e01af65710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0622ee20-cb57-540d-b982-16a006736a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369581} :answer-field/id #uuid "283b31ad-01df-58b4-a5fa-6e5bb8f366af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5ec0799-ee29-5297-a132-7bdd3f8273e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40889" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\text{ }\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99df3431-adba-550d-ae72-313470146593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132171} {:answer/id #uuid "cc592562-91ca-5ec5-8f8d-093bd990ff36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132172} {:answer/id #uuid "dac5b559-03eb-51ec-9c9a-3e9e8991a41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132173} {:answer/id #uuid "08a60458-7aac-5e30-a0de-661c967ff1b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132174} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb75956-c324-5a0d-bbf8-074c7d025a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc592562-91ca-5ec5-8f8d-093bd990ff36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132172} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13ab6eb-3fcb-5f1b-8f86-9be9948e9741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72ebe648-c9aa-5496-8f25-29cd029ad4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-41775" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=18 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=18 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8883179a-1a66-5e7b-883e-35e07309e2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369586} {:answer/id #uuid "04cb03e0-c3b5-5219-a135-2ee9b755ce8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369587} {:answer/id #uuid "0da6ac7e-d253-5250-8300-54797ad5c7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369588} {:answer/id #uuid "cd40a754-5b74-5bae-a24a-67e89a2fd24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369589} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba4c5ea-bf12-5243-bc71-20d573ed84fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{20} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da6ac7e-d253-5250-8300-54797ad5c7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369588} :answer-field/id #uuid "21176373-f903-59e5-a882-155719427294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82b0888f-f9b9-5cf6-bf2a-8bbdd3e5250a" :question/math-academy-id "q-41776" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f4ab31-f0b7-5497-80e3-fb24304cf316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369593} {:answer/id #uuid "9789b522-415e-5fe7-b7ca-0e66b652e811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369594} {:answer/id #uuid "6c75c309-0340-5b7e-bf38-a751a4f2eead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{14} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369595} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebd4fe1-e496-5b7f-afe2-cf485d818dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369596} {:answer/id #uuid "85e18d47-135c-5cb7-84d8-fed8918df6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9789b522-415e-5fe7-b7ca-0e66b652e811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369594} :answer-field/id #uuid "81358ba8-e7e1-5402-90a5-a43f3f6d6dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0174f23-e3c1-53ee-8e26-06192fa2ac03" :question/math-academy-id "q-41779" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{42}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6510e3a-24cc-5179-9565-c289e535cbd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{19}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369600} {:answer/id #uuid "817f957b-11ef-5513-bc21-bcd843bd3325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369601} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1ce12f-2ffa-502e-b026-6c1dbf21c1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{25} \\\\ \\frac{1}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369602} {:answer/id #uuid "6afe4427-7dfb-5789-a09c-bc5c14524f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ \\frac{11}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369603} {:answer/id #uuid "7282aeda-0aca-5e3a-b4cb-876511f05810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "817f957b-11ef-5513-bc21-bcd843bd3325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369601} :answer-field/id #uuid "49016c1d-96f0-5854-9063-c851d9d7e5b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eaedb1a-fe31-57bf-9817-79f22500b6f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41780" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 29\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}29 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd5b6a0-c978-5625-bde0-def409d433d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132189} {:answer/id #uuid "1d77ec79-6dcf-5ad5-b961-37121fa4a294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132190} {:answer/id #uuid "211d052f-94dc-5992-bef1-246d5777db10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132191} {:answer/id #uuid "382ac189-ec0a-51e3-8e71-f1b5a52048b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132192} {:answer/id #uuid "489c370c-f718-51aa-82bb-93dd6e087480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "211d052f-94dc-5992-bef1-246d5777db10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132191} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b64b46-d957-5cf3-b08a-7771bb0e472a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85fd6f83-baf3-5731-972e-5511c5fc2e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-41783" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6832d82-3ea3-579f-a7d0-a8b259a11801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369607} {:answer/id #uuid "2e906fbf-055f-5f38-afa0-d40aca125646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369608} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdb84f1-a0b6-5780-8f42-135565377390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369609} {:answer/id #uuid "881cc747-084f-5678-9b8f-e1d269cf79f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369610} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf01513-998a-56e1-a4f4-dbb966df7b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6832d82-3ea3-579f-a7d0-a8b259a11801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369607} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb53557-7307-5768-8cf5-2834b240c866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a38eea7-13c5-59fe-a820-3f0859b3b4fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-85275" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=12 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc4fb31-24ec-51f7-9cb2-9eafdf2274c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{141}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369614} {:answer/id #uuid "c19428d8-1bb1-5766-b675-3d05b9450946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{114}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369615} {:answer/id #uuid "8283d971-e39a-5eef-a1a5-2ee3d386f631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369616} {:answer/id #uuid "949d9cad-94d2-57b5-8cb1-9144e23bc938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369617} {:answer/id #uuid "f041f736-504c-56b6-b081-af4086f28b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c19428d8-1bb1-5766-b675-3d05b9450946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{114}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369615} :answer-field/id #uuid "a407edc2-1b06-59d9-be37-732b469e658f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "339e2c24-0f9a-526a-8596-427ddec81a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-85278" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-7)^{2}}=\\sqrt{114}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22428b20-a26b-5085-bb46-c01057a2fa7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369621} {:answer/id #uuid "384c4911-96bd-5d4f-915b-7e88f6c052b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369622} {:answer/id #uuid "2686c2af-97ef-53be-9b89-32d096597c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369623} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1b6853-f671-56b2-942a-ed305c9709fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369624} {:answer/id #uuid "dd538d59-aee9-5725-9d19-809e0e6efe7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd538d59-aee9-5725-9d19-809e0e6efe7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369625} :answer-field/id #uuid "39d66f4d-9368-5ee8-b3ff-937e9e295561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9a1e571-b70e-5954-9090-bf26c2e1d803" :question/math-academy-id "q-85280" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-11)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{71}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c9dcec6-947a-53af-8864-b9ad24b5f651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369628} {:answer/id #uuid "424a3d58-03b4-5418-a7c1-c0351c5c2656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369629} {:answer/id #uuid "b0826008-218c-5bd6-9a6e-6de0b4c48bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369630} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cb192f-b86d-5b23-9322-ec6f0c46a444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369631} {:answer/id #uuid "8c94b5d8-367e-578c-8769-b4fd4cf13df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{101}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0826008-218c-5bd6-9a6e-6de0b4c48bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369630} :answer-field/id #uuid "769a5227-f164-5a89-952a-4843116574bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d64d189-36ec-5faa-a1eb-5573a00d3bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-85284" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{110}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d48719af-3e19-58fb-8f69-9b3ad90e62c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369635} {:answer/id #uuid "a2873116-1dd8-553c-a30e-233275a88643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369636} {:answer/id #uuid "16fc3d0a-4f8e-59ed-88ff-31f32dd1c881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369637} {:answer/id #uuid "80e0ccff-0713-56d5-9053-5c8574242602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369638} {:answer/id #uuid "8524fa44-f2c1-5a82-840b-9403fa71c7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2873116-1dd8-553c-a30e-233275a88643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369636} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2972d8d-9c80-533c-91af-d3bd8dba8e50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "677d1334-79c7-532a-aa7f-e52d487bc38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85892" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7543d53-10f1-5e13-9891-41f3e4abaf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369642} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf32517-d0bc-5d98-a1b2-96fa0692da82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369643} {:answer/id #uuid "6322981b-006b-585f-8b0a-f890bad9a0fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369644} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf05e3b-ea0c-538c-b1e8-d64b712dfada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369645} {:answer/id #uuid "1991fce0-4909-56bb-a60f-4624a1da7b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1991fce0-4909-56bb-a60f-4624a1da7b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369646} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca91f941-7185-5839-9df1-58693da791a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c761d373-1b10-5d00-ac6a-15cc406ca2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-85895" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dac0bf64-cdf7-5fbd-aa35-e228e6f94d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369649} {:answer/id #uuid "f704dede-11e0-54e8-a2a1-6760d961f308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369650} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7ecf04-867c-54cd-8a51-3091dab2f353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369651} {:answer/id #uuid "7adf4828-22d6-5aa7-930f-3f838513f5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369652} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9b3d95-8c09-5f87-b94e-1fcaf4dc858c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f704dede-11e0-54e8-a2a1-6760d961f308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369650} :answer-field/id #uuid "9047fb30-b7f6-5b6f-be6b-cbc5b98b7d1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4687792-64f0-5adf-ae27-9d8b1b6a591b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85899" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "770a049a-9380-527b-b60a-101ce32be800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369656} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d63d07-7620-5b31-a4b1-c1044854d2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369657} {:answer/id #uuid "5410eac1-24d4-5bc8-a29c-9a371267e1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369658} {:answer/id #uuid "38c6ca89-f2e1-54c6-9be7-e228929c429d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369659} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a2eb2b-077c-574b-9d49-e28b74d5b70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8a2eb2b-077c-574b-9d49-e28b74d5b70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369660} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb21bdb7-8746-5c3d-8f14-318958ebb97b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b496936-916c-5846-ae6d-832becc23a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85909" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}]}
